WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:02.790
- [Announcer] If you want to
travel by Amtrak from Houston

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.850
to San Antonio, Texas,
you only have one option,

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:09.300
the Sunset Limited, a long
distance train making 22 stops

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.250
from New Orleans to Los Angeles.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.780
It only runs three times a week

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:13.980
and according to Amtrak

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.830
is over an hour and a
half late on average.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:18.300
Compare that to the Northeast,

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.640
where 40 Amtrak trains link Philadelphia

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.650
to New York every day.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:23.910
Amtrak has said for years

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.410
that it wants to replicate this model

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.360
in fast growing metropolitan areas,

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.030
like San Antonio, Phoenix, and Atlanta.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.530
But over the last 50 years,

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.440
the rail network has
remained largely unchanged.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.990
So why hasn't Amtrak expanded?

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.370
- So the biggest problem for Amtrak

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:39.870
in areas outside the Northeast

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.580
is that they don't control their own fate.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.830
They're running along
tracks that are owned

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.770
by the major freight
railroads of the country.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.600
- [Announcer] If Amtrak
wants to increase service

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.760
it needs to negotiate
with freight carriers.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.980
But those companies
have long resisted calls

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.190
to make more room on their
tracks for passenger trains.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.980
- Really, this just comes
down to an inevitable tension

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.950
when the freights and passenger
railroads have to share

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.900
a limited capacity of track.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.790
- [Announcer] The only
area where Amtrak owns most

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:07.980
of the tracks is in
the Northeast Corridor.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.180
- For that reason,

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.730
and because it's much more
heavily a passenger corridor

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.100
they're able to run a lot more
trains much more frequently

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.260
and that's why you see the
ridership in the Northeast

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.480
that you don't see in
other parts of the country.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.090
- Amtrak has been renting
tracks from freight companies

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:22.440
since its inception,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.900
that's because freight
rail used to move goods

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.490
and people on its tracks.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.350
But by the end of the 1960s-

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:29.460
- They were running outta money

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:32.610
and they were in particular
losing money carrying people.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.430
- [Announcer] So in 1970,
Congress bailed out the railroads

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:38.400
and created Amtrak to relieve
freights of the obligation

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.680
to provide passenger rail service.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:42.390
Part of the deal was that freight trains

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.120
would give Amtrak right
of way on its tracks.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.280
- Although the law requires
them to get out of the way

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.230
and practice, they don't.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:51.480
- [Announcer] Amtrak blames
freight rail for its delays

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.250
saying passenger trains have to wait

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:54.750
for freight trains to pass.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:55.830
According to Amtrak,

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:59.040
freight rail caused
passengers nearly 15,000 hours

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.350
of delays in 2021.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.390
Part of the problem is
that freight carriers

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.930
run longer trains, some
stretching over two miles.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.270
- If the train is too
long to fit all the way

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:10.800
onto that small siding,

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.360
then you're still gonna be in the way

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.520
of whatever train is behind you.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:16.770
- [Announcer] Amtrak says,
freight rail not only prevents it

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:18.780
from operating trains at higher speeds

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.270
but also makes it difficult
to add more trains

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.060
by demanding excessive
capital investments.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.940
- The freights and their
advocates in Washington

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:30.660
can get pretty frustrated about
the passenger rail advocates

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.510
not taking seriously enough
the work that they are doing

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:36.900
because so much of the
nation's commodities and goods

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.700
are moving around on the rails.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.110
- [Announcer] Privately
owned freight companies

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.540
spend more than 20 billion each year

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.310
maintaining and improving their tracks

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.740
according to the American
Association of Railroads,

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:50.130
a trade group that includes
Amtrak and freight carriers.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:53.010
The group says Amtrak and
freight rail share the same goals

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.320
of providing efficient
and reliable service

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.500
to both passengers and freight
customers across the country.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:59.587
- This is train 125.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:01.950
- The freight railroads also say

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:05.190
that they do a better job
making way for Amtrak trains

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.620
than Amtrak would make it sound.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.420
- [Announcer] But if the
law requires freight trains

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.690
to give priority to Amtrak,
why hasn't that been enforced?

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.190
- There really are two reasons.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:17.104
One is that Amtrak has been
essentially fighting to survive

00:03:17.104 --> 00:03:18.900
for most of its history,

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:19.950
fighting to survive both

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.270
because there are people in Congress

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.430
who would've loved to
have it just go away.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.730
Also because they just struggled
sort of budget to budget

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:28.980
to just keep the lights on.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.390
- [Announcer] That's partly why Amtrak

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.700
hasn't bought more tracks
from freight railroads,

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.010
because it would be
prohibitively expensive.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.920
- And also the freight
railroads need these networks

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.690
so they would not be in the mood

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:41.290
to give up any of this trackage.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:43.650
- [Announcer] The second reason Amtrak

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.480
hasn't really been successful pushing back

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.770
against freight companies

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:48.780
is that there hasn't been
a good legal structure

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.850
for Amtrak to assert its rights.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.020
- There was a railroad regulator

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:53.610
called the Interstate Commerce Commission,

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:56.610
which was winnowed down
and eventually eliminated.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:59.730
The ICC sometimes did take actions

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:01.980
to improve passenger traffic

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:06.150
but it was spotty over
the history of of Amtrak.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.520
- [Announcer] But things
started to change in 2008,

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:10.680
Congress passed a law that directed Amtrak

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.960
to help develop a set
of metrics and standards

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.510
to rate the on-time
performance of its trains.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:18.270
- This would be a way to
measure whether Amtrak trains

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:20.940
were delayed in a way that
they're not supposed to be

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.230
by freight trains.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:23.730
- [Announcer] After
years of legal wrangling

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:25.740
that led all the way to the Supreme Court,

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.680
the on-time metrics took
effect in October, 2021,

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.740
which gave Amtrak the ability
to begin building its case

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:33.063
against freight railroads.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.970
In December, 2022,

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:39.060
Amtrak filed a complaint
against Union Pacific Railroad,

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:42.030
its first ever use of the 2008 law.

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:45.510
In its complaint, Amtrak seized
on that Sunset Limited line,

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:47.640
its most delayed route in the country.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.430
- This is a sign of how
aggressive Amtrak is becoming

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.410
about trying to enforce its rights

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:54.180
and being willing to pick a big fight

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:55.590
with the freight companies.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.030
- [Announcer] A Union Pacific spokeswoman

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.970
said the Sunset route is a
critical Union Pacific artery,

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:02.580
and it is essential that
Union Pacific and Amtrak

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:05.010
collaborate to design a
schedule that is reasonable

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:05.913
and achievable.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.543
The other big change in Amtrak situation,

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.780
the 2021 Infrastructure Law.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:16.260
- This law is the most
significant investment in rail,

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.090
the most significant investment in rail

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:21.810
since we created Amtrak
about 50 years ago.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:24.150
- [Announcer] The railroad
received $22 billion

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:27.144
in direct aid and will also
have access to $44 billion

00:05:27.144 --> 00:05:29.910
aimed at boosting passenger rail service.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:32.130
- They are still and
will always be reliant

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:33.300
on federal subsidies

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.340
but their balance sheet is much stronger.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:38.250
They are replacing cars and locomotives

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:40.500
at a rate that's much higher
than anybody has seen.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:44.640
- [Announcer] In 2021, Amtrak
released its 15 year vision

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:47.160
which includes improving
25 existing routes

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:50.010
and adding 39 new ones across the US.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:52.440
Imagine trains connecting Phoenix, Tucson,

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:55.950
and Southern California, Dallas,
Houston, and San Antonio,

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.260
and Nashville, Atlanta, and Charlotte.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.840
Amtrak says this proposal
is a starting point

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.580
but the Federal Railroad Administration

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.740
will help determine the final plan.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:06.720
- They've done a lot of market research

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:08.040
over the past few years

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:12.150
about whether there's demand
for passenger rail like this.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.760
They also have been doing
a really aggressive job

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.770
of courting local officials.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:19.830
Amtrak points to that as
evidence that this will work.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:22.470
- Every study shows you you
get from point A to point B

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:26.430
on rail as faster than
you can on automobile.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.950
You don't take the car
you get in the train.

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:31.200
- [Announcer] But to make sure
customers keep choosing rail

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:33.090
over other transportation options,

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:36.870
Amtrak says it's new service
can't have hour long delays.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:38.370
The key to success,

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:40.594
a new arrangement with
the freight railroads.

00:06:40.594 --> 00:06:43.177
(upbeat music)

